## Text Elements, Lists & Links

**Text formatting elements**
Beyond `<p>` and `<h1>`–`<h6>`, HTML has inline tags for emphasizing or marking up text within a block.

```html
<strong>Important text</strong>   <!-- bold, semantic importance -->
<em>Emphasized text</em>          <!-- italic, semantic emphasis -->
<b>Bold text</b>                  <!-- bold, no semantic meaning -->
<i>Italic text</i>                <!-- italic, no semantic meaning -->
<small>Fine print</small>
<mark>Highlighted text</mark>
<del>Deleted/strikethrough text</del>
<sub>Subscript</sub> and <sup>Superscript</sup>
```

**`<strong>`/`<em>` vs `<b>`/`<i>**<strong>` and `<em>` carry meaning (screen readers announce them differently, search engines weigh them). `<b>` and `<i>` are purely visual — same look, no meaning. Prefer `<strong>`/`<em>` unless it's genuinely just styling.

**Line breaks and horizontal rules**

```html
<p>Line one<br>Line two</p>
<hr>
```

`<br>` = line break inside text. `<hr>` = a horizontal divider between sections. Both are void elements (no closing tag).

**Lists**

Unordered list (bullets):

```html
<ul>
  <li>HTML</li>
  <li>CSS</li>
  <li>JavaScript</li>
</ul>
```

Ordered list (numbered):

```html
<ol>
  <li>Wake up</li>
  <li>Learn HTML</li>
  <li>Build a website</li>
</ol>
```

Nested list:

```html
<ul>
  <li>Frontend
    <ul>
      <li>HTML</li>
      <li>CSS</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Backend</li>
</ul>
```

Description list (less common, but useful — term + definition pairs):

```html
<dl>
  <dt>HTML</dt>
  <dd>Markup language for structuring web pages</dd>
</dl>
```

**Links — the `<a>` tag**

```html
<a href="https://google.com">Go to Google</a>
```

`href` = the destination URL. This is required for the link to work.

Opening in a new tab:

```html
<a href="https://google.com" target="_blank">Google</a>
```

`target="_blank"` opens in a new tab. Without it, the link replaces the current page.

Linking to another page in the same project:

```html
<a href="about.html">About Us</a>
```

Relative path — no domain needed if the file is in the same folder.

Linking to a section on the same page (using `id`):

```html
<h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
...
<a href="#contact">Jump to Contact</a>
```

Email and phone links:

```html
<a href="mailto:someone@example.com">Email us</a>
<a href="tel:+911234567890">Call us</a>
```

**Common mistakes**

- Forgetting `https://` in external links — browser may treat it as a relative path and fail
- Using `<b>` when they mean "this is important" — should use `<strong>`
- Nesting `<li>` directly under `<div>` instead of `<ul>`/`<ol>` — invalid structure
- Broken anchor links because the `id` doesn't match the `href="#..."` exactly (case-sensitive)

